A1. A kind of business recently carried on in Tennessee. ~ MONKEY
A6. This precedes every bona fide September morn. ~ AUGUST
A11. Ninety-nine and forty-four one hundredths per cent. (Come on, you reformers!) ~ PURE
A12. Things that trains run on. (Nothing to do with schedule.) ~ RAILS
A13. A revenue officer's party. ~ RAID
A15. Widows may be grass, but they're never this. ~ GREEN
A17. A subtle k'nd of sarcasm. ~ IRONY
A19. A low note (This is quite different from a vulgar letter.) vulgar letter.) ~ RE
A21. The king of Bashan. ~ OG
A22. Name of a book by Rudyard Kipling. ~ KIM
A23. Wet Smacks (abbr.). ~ WS
A24. The kind of a man whose mistakes are buried (abbr.). ~ DR
A25. A pronoun of the third person. ~ SHE
A27. A clergyman's total of one and one. ~ ONE
A29. You don't have to sit for your portrait to be done in this. ~ OIL
A31. This is related to the fatted calf. (Ankle is a five-letter word.) ~ COW
A32. An exclamation of surprise, delight, pain, contempt, pity, or.what have you? ~ AH
A34. Dessert in the Army. ~ BEANS
A35. Our old friend the sun god. ~ RA
A36. Pertaining to birth. ~ NATAL
A38. A clause of a sentence. (Nothing like the kind that grows on a cat.) ~ COLON
A40. Something waiters are supposed to do. ~ SERVE
A44. What a man does when his wife starts talking. ~ QUITS
A47. That is (abbr.). ~ IE
A48. These can make about thirty-six holes in one pair of golf socks. ~ MOTHS
A50. Printer's measure. ~ EM
A51. This-is what three policemen with small feet are. ~ ODD
A53. The kind of a guy who lends you a couple of cart wheels till pay day. ~ PAL
A54. When this is used the son sets with difficulty. ~ ROD
A56. To be ill. ~ AIL
A58. Pronoun. ~ US
A59. Arabia (abbr.). ~ AR
A60. It takes a lot of digging to get this. ~ ORE
A61. This is another exclamation. (See No. 32 horizontal.) ~ OH
A63. An abbreviation for the title of the guarder of the "Golden Gate." ~ ST
A64. This is what men do when they have a nightmare. ~ DREAM
A66. This is what Sampson was because he brought down the house. ~ ACTOR
A69. This is always under foot. ~ SHOE
A71. Small bodies of water completely surrounded by land. ~ PONDS
A72. Manner or appearance. ~ MIEN
A74. The part of a flower that holds the pollen. ~ STAMEN
A75. Place for sailing schooners. ~ SALOON

D1. The cry of a Greek kitten. ~ MU
D2. Three-fifths of a musical instrument to the strains of which many a man has marched to battle. ~ ORG
D3. A famous fiddler. ~ NERO
D4. Before. ~ ERE
D5. One hundred per cent. Easterners. ~ YANKEES
D6. Marry in haste and repent with this ~ ALIMONY
D7. United States Regulars (abbr.). ~ USR
D8. Ash cans. ~ URNS
D9. What women do when they have something to say. ~ SAY
D10. A note of the diatonic scale. ~ TI
D11. Well-known tyer of knots. ~ PARSON
D14. Patron Saint of Tennessee. (Correct this sentence.) ~ DARWIN
D16. This makes people egotistical. ~ EGO
D18. This old bird seldom gets home till morning. ~ OWL
D20. Exclamation of the hard of hearing. ~ EH
D24. To execute. ~ DO
D26. Something girls would rather do than dance. ~ EAT
D28. Take notice (abbr.). ~ NB
D30. Part of the verb to be. ~ IS
D31. Famous electrical equestrian. ~ CAL
D33. This much of a loaf is better than no vacation at all. ~ HALVE
D35. The kind of a fellow who does everything and everybody. ~ ROGUE
D37. A citizen of Africa and Tennessee. ~ APE
D39. Where cats go at night. ~ OUT
D40. Something a fish peddler does when business is good. ~ SHOUTS
D41. To free. ~ RID
D42. This man was very wise, but he sure was a bear for punishment. ~ SOLOMON
D43. Some are silver and some are loose. ~ THREADS
D45. A girl's name. ~ IMA
D46. A harem-scarum fellow. ~ SULTAN
D48. Famous pastry cook. ~ MA
D49. In like manner. ~ SO
D52. Dutiful spouse or souse (abbr.). ~ DS
D53. Prefix made famous by Mr. Volstead. ~ PRE
D55. This goes with a dash. ~ DOT
D57. If you got No. 30 vertical this will be easy. ~ IS
D59. An enclosed space. ~ AREA
D62. Man. (Latin.) ~ HOMO
D64. A speck. ~ DOT
D65. This is always monkeying around. ~ APE
D67. Cracked Silician Artichokes. (Try and get 'em!) (Abbr.) ~ CSA
D68. Place where Spanish fish are found. ~ RIO
D70. An abbreviation for a place on the road to knowledge. ~ HS
D73. Half of a printer's measure. ~ EN


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
A17: A subtle k'nd of sarcasm. → kind (broken type prints as k'nd)
A66: This is what Sampson was because he brought down the house. → Samson
D67: Cracked Silician Artichokes. (Try and get 'em!) (Abbr.) → Sicilian

